A generic declaration can use the same code with more than one type. Here `A` stands for the concrete type a caller will use.

### A generic struct

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |
]

In a `Box of I32`, the field `value` holds an I32. In a `Box of String`, it holds a String.

### A generic function

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    identity type A |value A| -> A {
        return value
    }
]

The type parameter `A` connects the parameter `value` to the return type.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    word = identity("Moth")
]

The String argument determines `A` for this call, so `word` also holds a String.

### The placeholder is scoped

The parameter name is meaningful only inside that declaration. You cannot use `A` as a value or refer to it from another declaration.
